### How does the Kafka source work?

The source reads a Kafka topic partitioned by user id. You match source parallelism to partition count so each subtask owns a share of partitions. The source assigns timestamps and watermarks from event time so downstream windows work correctly.

```java
package com.bank.delivery;

public class KafkaSourceDesign {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Kafka topic: delivery-events");
        System.out.println("Partitions: 12 (partitioned by user_id)");
        System.out.println("Source parallelism: 12");
        System.out.println("  -> 1 partition per subtask");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Watermark strategy:");
        System.out.println("  BoundedOutOfOrderness(5 seconds)");
        System.out.println("  Idle partition timeout: 30 seconds");
    }
}
```

```text
Kafka topic: delivery-events
Partitions: 12 (partitioned by user_id)
Source parallelism: 12
  -> 1 partition per subtask

Watermark strategy:
  BoundedOutOfOrderness(5 seconds)
  Idle partition timeout: 30 seconds
```

### What is a forwarding exchange?

A forwarding exchange is the cheap, local data transfer between chained operators. Because the map enrichment does not change partitioning, Flink connects source to map with a forward: subtask n feeds subtask n with no network shuffle. The two chain into one task for efficiency.

```java
package com.bank.delivery;

public class ForwardingExchange {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Source subtask 0 -> Map subtask 0");
        System.out.println("Source subtask 1 -> Map subtask 1");
        System.out.println("Source subtask 2 -> Map subtask 2");
        System.out.println("... (no network shuffle)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Forwarding: local, cheap, chained.");
        System.out.println("Both operators run in one task slot.");
    }
}
```

```text
Source subtask 0 -> Map subtask 0
Source subtask 1 -> Map subtask 1
Source subtask 2 -> Map subtask 2
... (no network shuffle)

Forwarding: local, cheap, chained.
Both operators run in one task slot.
```

### What is a redistributing exchange (keyBy)?

keyBy performs a redistributing exchange: Flink hashes the user id to a key group and routes each record across the network to the subtask responsible for that key group. This is the main network cost, but it is what makes keyed state and per-user windows possible.

```java
package com.bank.delivery;

public class RedistributingExchange {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("keyBy(user_id):");
        System.out.println("  hash('user-A') % maxParallelism -> subtask 3");
        System.out.println("  hash('user-B') % maxParallelism -> subtask 7");
        System.out.println("  hash('user-C') % maxParallelism -> subtask 1");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Redistributing: network shuffle.");
        System.out.println("Records cross the network to reach");
        System.out.println("the subtask owning their key group.");
        System.out.println("This is the expensive step.");
    }
}
```

```text
keyBy(user_id):
  hash('user-A') % maxParallelism -> subtask 3
  hash('user-B') % maxParallelism -> subtask 7
  hash('user-C') % maxParallelism -> subtask 1

Redistributing: network shuffle.
Records cross the network to reach
the subtask owning their key group.
This is the expensive step.
```

### How does the tumbling window aggregate?

A 30-minute tumbling window groups each user's events into fixed, non-overlapping buckets by event time. An aggregate function computes per-user stats like event count, completed deliveries, and average scan interval. The window fires when the watermark passes the window's end.

```java
package com.bank.delivery;

public class TumblingWindowAggregate {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Tumbling window: 30 minutes");
        System.out.println("  [09:00-09:30) [09:30-10:00) ...");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Per-user aggregate at window close:");
        System.out.println("  user_id: user-A");
        System.out.println("  window_start: 09:00");
        System.out.println("  event_count: 8");
        System.out.println("  completed: 3");
        System.out.println("  avg_scan_interval_sec: 120");
    }
}
```

```text
Tumbling window: 30 minutes
  [09:00-09:30) [09:30-10:00) ...

Per-user aggregate at window close:
  user_id: user-A
  window_start: 09:00
  event_count: 8
  completed: 3
  avg_scan_interval_sec: 120
```

### How does the JDBC sink work?

The JDBC sink writes one row per user per window to PostgreSQL. You configure batching and set sink parallelism lower than source parallelism to respect database connection limits. The sink uses an upsert keyed by user and window start for idempotency.

```sql
-- JDBC sink: upsert per user per window
INSERT INTO delivery_stats
    (user_id, window_start, event_count, completed, avg_interval)
VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)
ON CONFLICT (user_id, window_start)
DO UPDATE SET event_count  = EXCLUDED.event_count,
              completed    = EXCLUDED.completed,
              avg_interval = EXCLUDED.avg_interval;
```

```java
package com.bank.delivery;

public class JdbcSinkDesign {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("JDBC sink config:");
        System.out.println("  Parallelism: 4 (lower than source 12)");
        System.out.println("  Batch size: 500 rows per flush");
        System.out.println("  Upsert key: (user_id, window_start)");
        System.out.println("  Idempotent: replay-safe");
    }
}
```

```text
JDBC sink config:
  Parallelism: 4 (lower than source 12)
  Batch size: 500 rows per flush
  Upsert key: (user_id, window_start)
  Idempotent: replay-safe
```

### How do parallelism settings work?

Parallelism is set per operator. Match source parallelism to partitions, give the keyed window enough parallelism to spread user keys, and keep the sink parallelism lower to respect database connection pools. Flink chains forwarding operators into tasks for efficiency.

```java
package com.bank.delivery;

public class ParallelismSettings {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Operator parallelism:");
        System.out.println("  Source (Kafka):    12 (= partitions)");
        System.out.println("  Map (enrich):      12 (forwarded/chained)");
        System.out.println("  keyBy + Window:    12 (spread keys)");
        System.out.println("  JDBC Sink:          4 (connection limit)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Task slots needed: 12");
        System.out.println("  Source+Map chained in 12 slots");
        System.out.println("  Window in 12 slots (after shuffle)");
        System.out.println("  Sink in 4 slots");
    }
}
```

```text
Operator parallelism:
  Source (Kafka):    12 (= partitions)
  Map (enrich):      12 (forwarded/chained)
  keyBy + Window:    12 (spread keys)
  JDBC Sink:          4 (connection limit)

Task slots needed: 12
  Source+Map chained in 12 slots
  Window in 12 slots (after shuffle)
  Sink in 4 slots
```

### How does checkpoint-based fault tolerance work?

Flink injects barriers into the stream. When a barrier reaches an operator, it snapshots state (Kafka offsets at the source, open window contents at the window) to durable storage. On failure, Flink restores the last checkpoint, resets Kafka to saved offsets, and replays. With an idempotent upsert sink, exactly-once is achieved.

```java
package com.bank.delivery;

public class CheckpointFaultTolerance {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Checkpoint flow:");
        System.out.println("  1. Barrier injected at source");
        System.out.println("  2. Flows through map (forwarded)");
        System.out.println("  3. Crosses keyBy (redistributed)");
        System.out.println("  4. Window snapshots open windows");
        System.out.println("  5. Sink acknowledges");
        System.out.println("  6. Checkpoint complete on S3/HDFS");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("On failure:");
        System.out.println("  Restore checkpoint -> replay Kafka");
        System.out.println("  -> window state correct");
        System.out.println("  -> upsert sink: no duplicates");
        System.out.println("  -> exactly-once end-to-end");
    }
}
```

```text
Checkpoint flow:
  1. Barrier injected at source
  2. Flows through map (forwarded)
  3. Crosses keyBy (redistributed)
  4. Window snapshots open windows
  5. Sink acknowledges
  6. Checkpoint complete on S3/HDFS

On failure:
  Restore checkpoint -> replay Kafka
  -> window state correct
  -> upsert sink: no duplicates
  -> exactly-once end-to-end
```

